      <span>•</span>
      <span>NEC NFPA 70 Section 250.32</span>
    </div>
    <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-black text-gray-900 tracking-tight mb-3">
      Why Neutrals and Grounds Must Be Separated in Subpanels (NEC 250.32)
    </h1>
    <p class="text-base text-gray-600 leading-relaxed max-w-3xl mb-6">
      One of the most frequently failed electrical inspection items in DIY workshop and basement wiring is the bonding of grounds and neutrals in a subpanel. While neutrals and grounds are bonded together at your main electrical meter panel, connecting them downstream creates a dangerous condition known as <em>objectionable parallel return current</em>.
    </p>

    <!-- Quick Stats Grid -->
    <div class="grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 pt-6 border-t border-gray-100">
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500">Main Panel</div>
        <div class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900 mt-0.5">Bonded (Green Screw IN)</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500">Subpanel</div>
        <div class="text-sm font-bold text-emerald-600 mt-0.5">Floating (Green Screw OUT)</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500">Required Feed</div>
        <div class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900 mt-0.5">4-Wire Feeder (2 Hots, N, G)</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Main Editorial Content -->
  <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6 sm:p-8 mb-8 prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">What Does the Neutral Wire Actually Do?</h2>
    <p>
      In an alternating current (AC) electrical system, electricity leaves the main panel on the hot conductor (120V), powers the appliance, and returns on the neutral conductor to complete the circuit. <strong>The neutral wire is a normal current-carrying conductor.</strong> Under ordinary operation, amperes are constantly flowing through it.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8">What Does the Equipment Ground Wire Do?</h2>
    <p>
      The equipment grounding conductor (bare copper or green insulated wire) is an <strong>emergency fault path</strong>. Under normal conditions, it should carry <em>zero current</em>. Its sole purpose is to provide a low-resistance path back to the panel to instantly trip a circuit breaker if a live hot wire touches a metal appliance chassis.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8">The Physics of "Parallel Current" Shock Hazards</h2>
    <p>
      If you install the green bonding screw in a subpanel, you bridge the neutral bar and the ground bar together. Because electricity takes <em>all</em> parallel paths inversely proportional to resistance, returning current from your 120V tools will split:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Roughly half the return current travels along the insulated white neutral wire.</li>
      <li>The other half travels along the bare equipment grounding wire, through the metal subpanel enclosure, through metal conduits, water pipes, and the metal frames of connected shop tools!</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      If the subpanel neutral wire ever becomes loose or disconnected, <strong>100% of the return current travels through your equipment grounds</strong>. Touching the metal case of your drill press, table saw, or washing machine can deliver a lethal 120V electric shock.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8">The 4-Wire Subpanel Mandate (NEC 250.32(B))</h2>
    <p>
      Since the 2008 National Electrical Code update, all feeders to detached garages, sheds, or workshops must contain <strong>four separate conductors</strong>:
    </p>
    <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-xl p-5 border border-gray-200 my-4 not-prose">
      <ul class="text-xs text-gray-700 space-y-2 mb-0">
        <li><strong>Conductor 1 (Hot Phase A):</strong> Black wire connected to subpanel lug A.</li>
        <li><strong>Conductor 2 (Hot Phase B):</strong> Red wire connected to subpanel lug B.</li>
        <li><strong>Conductor 3 (Neutral):</strong> White wire connected to isolated/floating neutral bar.</li>
        <li><strong>Conductor 4 (Equipment Ground):</strong> Bare or green wire connected to direct-bonded chassis ground bar.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Jobsite Field Note -->
  <FieldNote
    author="Marcus Vance"
    role="Master Electrician & Building Systems Specialist"
    experience="22+ Years Field Experience"
    avatarBg="bg-amber-100 text-amber-800"
    initials="MV"
    title="How Inspectors Test for Floating Neutrals in 5 Seconds"
    note="When a city electrical inspector opens your newly installed subpanel cover, they don't even look at the branch circuits first. They pull out a digital multimeter, set it to continuity (ohms), touch one probe to the neutral busbar and the other probe to the painted metal enclosure. If the multimeter beeps, you fail instantly. The neutral bar must be 100% isolated on plastic standoff blocks from the metal enclosure. Always inspect your panel box when opening it from the store: many manufacturers ship panels with the green bonding screw sitting loose in a plastic bag—leave it in the bag or throw it in the trash!"
  />

  <!-- Costly Mistakes -->
  <CostlyMistakes
    tradeName="Subpanel Electrical Installation"
    mistakes={[
      {
        number: 1,
        title: "Leaving the Factory Neutral Tie-Bar Connected to Enclosure",
        costImpact: "$1,200 (Failed Inspection & Service Call)",
        description: "Some commercial subpanels come with a factory copper cross-strap bridging the left and right busbars to the cabinet. Failing to remove this link energizes the box.",
        solution: "Inspect the back of both busbars and verify that the neutral assembly rests entirely on raised non-conductive plastic insulating insulators."
      },
      {
        number: 2,
        title: "Omitting Supplemental Ground Rods at Detached Buildings",
        costImpact: "$650 (Failed Final Inspection)",
        description: "NEC 250.32(A) requires detached structures fed by a subpanel to have their own grounding electrode system (two 5/8\" x 8ft copper ground rods spaced 6ft apart).",
        solution: "Drive two 8-foot ground rods into the earth at the detached workshop and bond them with 6 AWG bare copper wire directly to the subpanel grounding bar."
      },
      {
        number: 3,
        title: "Mixing Neutral and Ground Wires in the Same Lug Hole",
        costImpact: "$350 (Failed Rough-In Inspection)",
        description: "While NEC allows up to two equipment ground wires of the same gauge under a single terminal screw, neutral wires must NEVER share a hole with another conductor.",
        solution: "Always terminate every white neutral wire under its own dedicated terminal screw on the isolated neutral busbar."
      }
    ]}
  />

  <!-- Builder Community Reviews -->
